### Dense retrieval in Postgres (pgvector, HNSW)

All 1,520 tools embedded (100-d GloVe, SIF weighting) and stored in Postgres with pgvector 0.6 and an HNSW index (m = 16, ef_construction = 64, built in 0.38 s). Over 1,911 queries: the approximate top 10 shares 99.2% of its entries with exact cosine search; recall@5 of the correct tool 65.0% (exact: 65.3%); query latency p50 0.61 ms, p99 1.80 ms (in-process brute force over 1,520 vectors: p50 0.48 ms).

## 2. Skill triggering as the skill count grows

Each catalog tool plays an Agent Skill (its name and description are the tier-1 text that is always in context). Relevant queries have one matching skill; BFCL irrelevance queries have none. The trigger loads the top skill when its trigram similarity clears a threshold chosen on a training half at N = 100 (train F1 0.736) and kept fixed. This is a retrieval trigger, not a model's decision.

| skills | precision | recall | false triggers on irrelevant queries | tier-1 tokens for all skills |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 10 | 89.2% | 84.3% | 13.9% | 227 |
| 50 | 83.6% | 72.4% | 17.7% | 1,140 |
| 100 | 76.4% | 65.7% | 24.3% | 2,272 |
| 200 | 67.9% | 61.6% | 34.2% | 4,538 |
| 500 | 52.2% | 52.0% | 48.5% | 11,348 |
| 1,000 | 43.6% | 48.1% | 62.7% | 22,710 |
| 1,520 | 38.3% | 43.5% | 67.7% | 34,506 |

